# RISC0 Guest Program Standards

<version>1.2.1</version>

## Context
- When implementing RISC0 guest programs that run inside the zkVM
- When handling structured output in guest programs
- When formatting proof outputs for verification
- When managing guest program memory and resources
- When implementing zero-knowledge computations

## Requirements

### Guest Program Structure
```rust
#![no_main]  // Required for guest programs
#![no_std]   // Required unless specifically needing std

use risc0_zkvm::guest::env;

risc0_zkvm::guest::entry!(main);  // Required entry point macro

fn main() {
    // 1. Read inputs - Use appropriate type
    let private_input: T = env::read();    // Private inputs
    let public_input: U = env::read();     // Public inputs if needed
    
    // 2. Process inputs safely
    let result = match compute(private_input, public_input) {
        Ok(result) => result,
        Err(e) => {
            // Handle errors appropriately
            commit_error(e);
            return;
        }
    };
    
    // 3. Commit outputs in standard format
    commit_structured_output(result);
}

// Always implement error handling
fn commit_error(error: ComputeError) {
    let mut error_output = vec![0xFF];  // Error marker
    error_output.extend_from_slice(error.as_bytes());
    env::commit_slice(&error_output);
}
```

### Output Protocol
1. Input Commitments:
```rust
// Always commit input hashes first
env::commit_slice(&hash_inputs(&private_input));
env::commit_slice(&hash_inputs(&public_input));
```

2. Structured Output Format:
```rust
// Standard marker bytes
const MARKER_SUCCESS: u8 = 0xAA;
const MARKER_ERROR: u8 = 0xFF;
const MARKER_DEBUG: u8 = 0xBB;

fn commit_structured_output<T: Serialize>(result: T) {
    // 1. Marker byte
    let mut output = vec![MARKER_SUCCESS];
    
    // 2. Serialized data
    output.extend(bincode::serialize(&result).unwrap());
    
    // 3. Commit
    env::commit_slice(&output);
}
```

3. Debug/Human-Readable Output (Optional):
```rust
fn commit_debug_info(info: &str) {
    let mut debug = vec![MARKER_DEBUG];
    debug.extend_from_slice(info.as_bytes());
    env::commit_slice(&debug);
}
```

### Memory Management Guidelines
```rust
// 1. Use fixed buffers for known sizes
let mut buffer = [0u8; 1024];  // Fixed size when possible

// 2. Process large inputs in chunks
fn process_large_input(input: &[u8]) {
    for chunk in input.chunks(1024) {
        process_chunk(chunk);
        // Clear any temporary data
        clear_sensitive_data();
    }
}

// 3. Clear sensitive data
fn clear_sensitive_data() {
    // Zero out sensitive buffers
    buffer.iter_mut().for_each(|b| *b = 0);
}

// 4. Avoid recursive algorithms
// ❌ Bad: Recursive implementation
fn recursive_process(data: &[u8]) {
    if data.is_empty() { return; }
    process_byte(data[0]);
    recursive_process(&data[1..]);  // Stack usage grows
}

// ✅ Good: Iterative implementation
fn iterative_process(data: &[u8]) {
    for &byte in data {
        process_byte(byte);
    }
}
```

### Error Handling
```rust
// 1. Define custom error types
#[derive(Debug)]
enum GuestError {
    InvalidInput,
    ComputationError,
    MemoryLimit,
}

// 2. Implement error handling
fn handle_computation() -> Result<(), GuestError> {
    let input: Vec<u8> = env::read();
    
    if input.len() > MAX_INPUT_SIZE {
        commit_error(GuestError::MemoryLimit);
        return Err(GuestError::MemoryLimit);
    }
    
    // Process and handle errors
    match compute_result(input) {
        Ok(result) => {
            commit_structured_output(result);
            Ok(())
        }
        Err(e) => {
            commit_error(e);
            Err(e)
        }
    }
}
```

## Examples

<example>
```rust
// Good: Complete guest program with proper structure
#![no_main]
#![no_std]

use risc0_zkvm::guest::env;
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};

#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
struct ComputationResult {
    hash: [u8; 32],
    result: Vec<u8>,
}

risc0_zkvm::guest::entry!(main);

fn main() {
    // 1. Read and validate input
    let input: Vec<u8> = env::read();
    if !validate_input(&input) {
        commit_error(GuestError::InvalidInput);
        return;
    }

    // 2. Compute result
    let result = match compute(&input) {
        Ok(r) => r,
        Err(e) => {
            commit_error(e);
            return;
        }
    };

    // 3. Commit outputs in order
    // a. Input hash
    env::commit_slice(&hash_inputs(&input));
    
    // b. Structured result
    let mut output = vec![MARKER_SUCCESS];
    output.extend(bincode::serialize(&result).unwrap());
    env::commit_slice(&output);
    
    // c. Optional debug info
    if cfg!(debug_assertions) {
        commit_debug_info(&format!("Processed {} bytes", input.len()));
    }
}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
```rust
// Bad: Incorrect guest program structure
#![no_main]

use std::vec::Vec;  // ❌ Wrong: Using std
use risc0_zkvm::guest::env;

fn main() {  // ❌ Wrong: Missing entry macro
    // ❌ Wrong: Unstructured output
    env::commit_slice(b"result");  // No marker byte
    
    // ❌ Wrong: Recursive processing
    recursive_process(&input);
    
    // ❌ Wrong: No error handling
    let result = unsafe_compute();  // No error handling
}
```
</example>

<critical>
- ALWAYS use #![no_main] and #![no_std]
- ALWAYS use risc0_zkvm::guest::entry!(main)
- ALWAYS commit input hashes first
- ALWAYS use marker bytes for structured output
- ALWAYS implement proper error handling
- NEVER use recursive algorithms
- NEVER commit unstructured output
- NEVER exceed memory limits
- ALWAYS clear sensitive data after use
- ALWAYS validate input sizes before processing
</critical> 